---
// /accessibility/: the registry's accessibility statement (night phase 15). It describes what is in
// place and how to use it, and it must stay true of the code (CLAUDE.md, the launch pages): a
// feature named here exists. science.css only; it takes the platform's name from src/config.ts.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_FULL_NAME, SITE_NAME } from "../config";
---
<Base
  title="Accessibility"
  description="How the registry works for keyboard and screen-reader users: the standard it aims for, what is in place, and how to use it."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Accessibility" }]}
>
  <h1>Accessibility</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    {SITE_FULL_NAME} is built to be read and used without a mouse, without colour, and with a screen
    reader. This page says what that means in practice, and where it still falls short.
  </p>

  <h2>The standard we aim for</h2>
  <p>
    We aim for the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG, the common rules for accessible web
    pages) version 2.2, level AA. This is a goal, not a certificate: we test as we build, and we fix
    what we find. If a page fails one of these rules for you, it is a bug, and you can report it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in place</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Skip to the content.</strong> The first thing the keyboard reaches on every page is a
      "Skip to the content" link, so you jump past the masthead straight to the page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Landmarks and one heading tree.</strong> Each page has a banner, a navigation, one main
      region and a footer, and a single top heading, so a screen reader can move by region and by
      heading.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard shortcuts.</strong> Press <kbd>?</kbd> for the full list. Single-key shortcuts
      match the ones researchers know from other forges, and can be turned off in your preferences;
      the help and the command palette work either way.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The command palette.</strong> Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> (or <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd>)
      to jump to a page or run a command. It is a listbox you drive with the arrow keys and
      <kbd>Enter</kbd>, announced to a screen reader, and it asks the server for nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Visible focus.</strong> Whatever has the keyboard's focus shows a clear outline, in
      every theme. The "More contrast" preference thickens it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Preferences for how it looks.</strong> In <a href="/settings/preferences/">your preferences</a>:
      a dark theme (never forced on you; light is the default), more contrast, colour-vision palettes
      for the charts and coloured marks, reduced motion, link underlines, line spacing, tab size and a
      fixed-width font for rendered text. They are kept in your browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reduced motion.</strong> The site carries almost no animation, and it honours your
      system's reduced-motion setting on its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It reflows.</strong> Pages work down to a narrow phone (320 pixels wide) without a
      sideways scroll, and when you enlarge the text to 200 percent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Charts are also tables.</strong> Every chart {SITE_NAME} draws is a plain inline drawing
      with a text label, and the same numbers are given just below as a table you can read and copy,
      so nothing depends on seeing the shape or telling two colours apart.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mathematics and diagrams.</strong> Formulas are written as MathML, which the browser and
      the screen reader read natively, not as pictures. Diagrams carry a text description.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No colour-only meaning.</strong> A status is said in words ("code verified", "dead
      link"), never by a colour alone, and never as a decorative pill.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to drive it from the keyboard</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><kbd>Tab</kbd></dt><dd>move through the links and controls; the first stop is "Skip to the content".</dd>
    <dt><kbd>?</kbd></dt><dd>open the list of keyboard shortcuts.</dd>
    <dt><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> or <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd></dt><dd>open the command palette.</dd>
    <dt><kbd>/</kbd></dt><dd>jump to the search box.</dd>
    <dt><kbd>Escape</kbd></dt><dd>close the palette or a dialog.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Where it still falls short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      If you chose the dark theme, a page may show for an instant in light before it switches, because
      the registry serves one set of pages to everyone and applies your theme in your browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      The code published by the authors is shown as its authors wrote it: a script with poor contrast
      or a missing text alternative is theirs, and {SITE_NAME} does not rewrite it.
    </li>
    <li>
      A few of the smaller coloured marks are tuned for the light theme first; the colour-vision
      palettes cover the charts and the main marks.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Report an accessibility problem</h2>
  <p>
    If a page is hard or impossible to use, tell us: open the <a href="/report/">report page</a> and
    say which page and what went wrong. The registry collects no email address, so there is nothing to
    fill in about yourself. We treat an accessibility barrier as a bug to fix.
  </p>
</Base>
